        "howNativesThink": "Native English speakers in financial and operational contexts make a sharp distinction between working hard and working efficiently. While 'hard work' implies exertion, 'efficiency' implies elegance, waste elimination, and systemic optimization. In the native corporate psyche, efficiency is inextricably linked with Peter Drucker's maxim that 'efficiency is doing things right, while effectiveness is doing the right things.' When a native executive calls a process efficient, they mean it achieves results with zero friction, zero redundant headcount, and minimal cash burn.",
