        "howNativesThink": "To native English speakers in banking, real estate, and finance, 'endorsement' triggers a concrete physical and legal reflex: turning a check over to inspect the signature line, bank stamp, or the restrictive phrase 'For Deposit Only.' In commercial paper law, native speakers recognize that signing the back of a draft changes who is legally entitled to cash it and imposes secondary liability on the signer. Outside of commercial banking, native executives think of an insurance endorsement as a formal 'rider' that adds protection (like cyber liability or flood coverage) to an existing commercial policy, or as a career-defining public validation when an influential board member or industry leader endorses a strategic initiative.",
