        "evolution": "In medieval English common law (c. 13th-15th century), when a grantor executed a deed or bond to be held by an independent third party until the grantee fulfilled a covenant, the provisional document was written on a parchment strip or scroll termed an 'escrow' (Anglo-French 'escrove'). The deed remained merely an 'escrow'\u2014a conditional piece of parchment with no legal force\u2014until the condition was satisfied, at which point it was delivered to become a binding deed. Over centuries of Anglo-American commercial jurisprudence, the term broadened from denoting the physical deed scroll to designating the contractual custody mechanism and the segregated trust account holding money, deeds, or intellectual property pending transaction settlement.",
