        "culturalContext": "In Anglo-American economics, central bank benchmark interest rates (set by the Federal Reserve, Bank of England, or European Central Bank) are the primary macroeconomic lever steering commercial borrowing, consumer demand, corporate capital expenditure, and currency valuations. In TOEIC Parts 3, 4, and 7, dialogues and passages routinely feature corporate borrowing negotiations, interest rate fluctuations, and corporate conflict-of-interest compliance policies.",
        "howNativesThink": "To native business executives, 'interest' is the financial expression of the 'time value of money'. A dollar today is worth more than a dollar tomorrow; interest compensates the lender for inflation, deferred consumption, and default risk. In corporate management, executives constantly differentiate between interest expense (the drag of debt servicing on net profit) and interest income (returns on idle cash reserves). Concurrently, corporate attorneys and directors stay vigilant regarding an 'interest' in a commercial entity\u2014distinguishing a 'controlling interest' (holding majority voting rights) from a passive 'minority interest', and rigorously guarding against any 'conflict of interest' that violates fiduciary duty.",
        "mentalImage": "A corporate treasurer analyzing a debt amortization spreadsheet on an office monitor, comparing monthly principal repayments against fluctuating interest expenses under newly announced central bank policy rates.",
        "whenNotToUse": "Do not confuse with 'principal' (the original base sum borrowed or invested before interest accumulates), 'dividend' (a discretionary profit distribution paid to equity shareholders), or 'fee' (a flat administrative transaction charge). Do not use 'interest' to describe an operating profit margin.",
