        "commonMistakes": "Learners unfamiliar with legal terminology often mispronounce the word phonetically as /lj\u025b\u0303/ or like 'lion' (/\u02c8la\u026a.\u0259n/). In standard English legal and business practice, it is pronounced monosyllabically as /li\u02d0n/ (homophonous with 'lean'), though a two-syllable variant /\u02c8li\u02d0.\u0259n/ ('LEE-en') is also recognized in older references. Another frequent error is confusing 'lien' with 'loan' or 'mortgage': while a loan is the debt obligation and a mortgage is the security instrument, a 'lien' is the overarching legal right or claim encumbering specific property until that obligation is satisfied.",
