        "commonMistakes": "Non-native speakers frequently misplace the primary stress onto the second syllable (*mo-NE-ta-ry* or *mo-NE-tri*) by false analogy with words like proprietary or hereditary; in standard English, the primary stress sits squarely on the initial syllable MON-. Conceptually, learners often conflate monetary with fiscal. In economics and corporate finance, monetary refers strictly to currency, the money supply, and central bank actions (e.g., interest rate adjustments and open-market operations), whereas fiscal pertains specifically to government revenues, taxation, and public budgetary expenditures. Another error is using monetary in place of financial to describe routine personal budgeting (e.g., *my monetary budget* instead of *my financial budget*).",
