        "commonMistakes": "Learners frequently confuse 'profit' with its exact homophone 'prophet' (/\u02c8pr\u0251\u02d0.f\u026at/, a religious seer or spiritual messenger); while phonetically identical in listening passages, their orthography and semantic realms are completely distinct. In speech, non-native speakers often over-enunciate the second syllable as a full vowel /-i\u02d0t/ or /-e\u026at/ rather than using the standard reduced vowel /\u026a/ or /\u0259t/. In financial analysis, learners commonly conflate 'profit' with 'revenue' (total sales before expenses) or 'cash flow' (liquid funds entering/exiting), overlooking that a business can be profitable on an accrual basis while experiencing severe cash shortfalls.",
