        "commonMistakes": "Learners frequently mispronounce the primary vowel /u\u02d0/ as an open vowel /\u028c/ (*PRUH-dense*) or introduce an extraneous palatal glide /ju\u02d0/ (*PYROO-dense*); in standard English, after /pr/, it is articulated as a smooth, rounded /u\u02d0/ ('PROO-dense'). The second syllable should be reduced to an unstressed /d\u0259ns/ or syllabic [dn\u0329s], not an exaggerated full vowel. Conceptually, non-native speakers often mistake 'prudence' for excessive hesitation, cowardice, or penny-pinching parsimony. In corporate finance and law, 'prudence' is a positive virtue denoting disciplined foresight, sound judgment, and risk-weighted decision-making. In accounting, the 'prudence principle' (also called conservatism) mandates that financial statements do not overstate assets/income or understate liabilities/expenses.",
        "ipa": "/\u02c8pru\u02d0.d\u0259ns/",
