        "evolution": "The commercial use of 'return' to describe the proceeds or yield arriving from commercial trade dates back to 16th-century English maritime commerce, where merchant ships returned with sold cargo profits. The term 'investment' evolved from medieval feudal investitures (clothing an appointee with official authority or land tenure) into the financial commitment of money to generate interest or dividends by the 1740s. In 1914, American financial manager Donaldson Brown invented the DuPont analysis model for the DuPont Corporation, mathematically defining ROI as Profit Margin multiplied by Asset Turnover. This breakthrough established 'return on investment' as a foundational quantitative yardstick across corporate finance, capital expenditure rationing, and modern managerial accounting throughout the 20th and 21st centuries.",
        "firstRecordedUse": "Early 20th century (formally developed in corporate financial accounting by DuPont in 1914; widely published in industrial management literature by the 1920s)",
