        "howNativesThink": "Native English finance professionals never confuse financial 'spread' with bread spreads or decorative table spreads; in market contexts, 'spread' is instantly recognized as a mathematical margin, a buffer of risk compensation, or a pricing wedge. If an asset has a 'tight spread,' traders know it is liquid, cheap to transact, and heavily traded; if the spread is 'wide' or 'blowing out,' it warns of panic, illiquidity, or substantial credit risk.",
